stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> Running: Kubernetes starts the Deployment container

    state "Worker supervisor + independent timeout wrapper" as Running {
        [*] --> Startup
        Startup --> ClaimCutoff: Clean workspace and initialize health server
        ClaimCutoff --> Poll: Uptime below 900 seconds
        ClaimCutoff --> Draining: Uptime at least 900 seconds
        Poll --> Idle: HTTP 204
        Idle --> ClaimCutoff: Interruptible 5-second wait with heartbeat
        Poll --> Retry: API failure or invalid response
        Retry --> ClaimCutoff: Exponential retry with jitter and heartbeat
        Poll --> Attempt: Claimed job, even if response crosses the cutoff
        Attempt --> Cleanup: Child reports completion and exits
        Attempt --> ReportFailure: Child crashes or processing budget expires
        ReportFailure --> Cleanup: Kill process group, bounded failure-report child
        Cleanup --> ClaimCutoff: Reap child and clean workspace
        Draining --> CleanExit: Active attempt already finished

        note right of Attempt
            One fresh process per attempt.
            Whole-attempt deadline is the smaller
            of local job budget and server lease.
            Reserve up to 5 seconds for failure reporting.
            At minute 15, readiness becomes false,
            the active attempt continues until its deadline.
        end note

        note right of Retry
            Dependency failures affect readiness,
            while the supervisor remains live.
        end note
    }

    Running --> Cancelling: SIGTERM or SIGINT, including watchdog TERM at 1200 seconds
    Cancelling --> CleanExit: Stop claims, kill active process group, reap and clean up
    Cancelling --> ForcedExit: Watchdog KILL after 30-second grace if still running
    Running --> ForcedExit: Fatal failure or Kubernetes termination after grace
    CleanExit --> Restart: Container exits, normal recycling exits 0
    ForcedExit --> Restart: Unreported attempts recover through backend lease expiry
    Restart --> [*]: Kubernetes restarts the container according to its restart policy

    note left of Running
        Kubelet probes local HTTP endpoints:
        /startupz = initialization complete
        /livez = recent supervisor progress
        /readyz = successful queue response and not draining
        Idle and busy workers remain healthy.
        HTTP server responsiveness alone is insufficient.
    end note
